Day 7
Principles of Security

(TryHackMe – Security Fundamentals)

🛡️ Task 1: Introduction

Security is not a single control or tool — it is a layered approach.

🔐 Defence in Depth

Defence in Depth means applying multiple, varied layers of security to systems and data so that if one layer fails, others still protect the asset.

Examples of layers:

Network security (firewalls, IDS)

Application security

Access control

Monitoring & logging

Policies & training

👉 Goal: Redundancy + resilience

🔺 Task 2: The CIA Triad

The CIA Triad is a foundational information security model used when designing security policies.

🔁 CIA is a Cycle

If any one element fails, the others become ineffective.

Element	Purpose
Confidentiality	Prevent unauthorised access
Integrity	Prevent unauthorised modification
Availability	Ensure data is accessible when needed
🔐 Confidentiality

Protects data from unauthorised disclosure.

Examples:

HR records only accessible to HR

Government classification (Top Secret, Classified)

Access control & encryption

🧾 Integrity

Ensures data remains accurate and unaltered unless authorised.

Threats:

Unauthorised modification

Accidental changes

System errors

Controls:

Access control

Hashing

Digital signatures

Authentication

⚡ Availability

Ensures systems & data are available when needed.

Key techniques:

Redundant servers

Failover systems

DDoS protection

SLAs (e.g. 99.99% uptime)

✅ Task 2 Answers

Prevent unauthorised modification → Integrity

Ensure data is available → Availability

Restrict access → Confidentiality

🔑 Task 3: Principles of Privileges

Access must be controlled based on:

User role

Data sensitivity

🔐 Principle of Least Privilege

Users should only have the minimum access required to perform their job.

🧩 PIM vs PAM
Concept	Meaning	Purpose
PIM	Privileged Identity Management	Maps job role → system role
PAM	Privileged Access Management	Manages what privileges the role has

PAM also includes:

Password management

Auditing

Reducing attack surface

✅ Task 3 Answers

PIM full form → Privileged Identity Management

PAM full form → Privileged Access Management

Manage role privileges → PAM

Create role based on job → PIM

🧱 Task 4: Security Models Continued

Security models formally enforce CIA principles.

🛡️ Bell-LaPadula Model (Confidentiality)

Rule:

No Read Up, No Write Down

Access based on clearance

Need-to-know basis

Used in:

Military

Government

Pros

Simple & proven

Matches hierarchy

Cons

Requires high trust

Object existence still visible

🧪 Biba Model (Integrity)

Rule:

No Read Down, No Write Up

Prevents data corruption

Prioritises integrity over confidentiality

Used in:

Software development

Environments where correctness matters

Pros

Strong integrity guarantees

Cons

Can slow workflows

Many access levels to manage

✅ Task 4 Answers

“No read up” model → Bell-LaPadula

“No read down” model → Biba

Military use → Bell-LaPadula

Software dev use → Biba

