Day 13
Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF)
📌 Overview

SSRF (Server-Side Request Forgery) is a vulnerability that allows an attacker to force the web server to make HTTP requests to internal or external resources of the attacker's choosing.

Instead of the attacker directly accessing internal resources,
👉 the server does it on behalf of the attacker.

🧠 What is SSRF?

SSRF stands for:

Server-Side Request Forgery


It occurs when:

A web application fetches a resource based on user-supplied input

That input is not properly validated

The attacker manipulates the request destination

🔥 Types of SSRF
1️⃣ Regular SSRF

Response is returned to attacker

Easier to detect

Data visible in browser

2️⃣ Blind SSRF

No response returned

Requires external monitoring

Harder to detect

Uses tools like:

Burp Collaborator

requestbin

Custom HTTP listener

🚨 Impact of SSRF

Successful SSRF can lead to:

Access to internal services

Access to admin panels

Exposure of credentials/tokens

Cloud metadata leakage

Internal network scanning

Privilege escalation

🧪 SSRF Example Flag
THM{SSRF_MASTER}

🔎 Finding SSRF Vulnerabilities

Common places to look:

1️⃣ Full URL parameter
?url=https://example.com/file

2️⃣ Hidden form fields
3️⃣ Hostname parameter
?srv=filestorage.cloud.thm

4️⃣ Partial path input
🎯 Most Likely Vulnerable Example
https://website.thm/fetch-file.php?fname=242533.pdf&srv=filestorage.cloud.thm&port=8001


Reason:

User controls hostname

User controls port

Server likely makes backend request

🛡️ SSRF Defenses & Bypass Techniques

Security-aware developers may implement:

🔴 Deny List

Blocks specific domains/IPs like:

localhost
127.0.0.1
169.254.169.254

🚨 Bypass Techniques

Alternative localhost references:

