Day 16
Command Injection
📌 Overview

Command Injection is a web vulnerability where an attacker can execute operating system commands through a vulnerable application.

This occurs when:

User input is passed directly to system commands
without proper validation or sanitization.

Because of this, attackers can execute commands on the server with the same privileges as the application.

Example:

whoami

This command reveals which user account the application is running as.

Command Injection is often considered a form of:

Remote Code Execution (RCE)

because attackers can remotely run commands on the system.

🧠 How Command Injection Works

Many web applications execute system commands using programming functions.

Examples:

PHP
exec()
system()
passthru()
Python
subprocess.run()
os.system()

If user input is included inside these commands, an attacker can inject additional commands.

🧪 Example Vulnerable Code (PHP)
$title = $_GET['title'];
system("grep $title songtitle.txt");

Steps:

1️⃣ User enters a song title
2️⃣ Application stores input in $title
3️⃣ The command searches a file using grep
4️⃣ Output is returned to the user

Vulnerability

If the attacker enters:

Beatles; whoami

The executed command becomes:

grep Beatles songtitle.txt; whoami

Now the system runs two commands.

⚔ Command Chaining Operators

Attackers use shell operators to run multiple commands.

Operator	Function
;	Execute another command
&&	Run next command if first succeeds
&	Run commands in background
`	`

Example:

ls; whoami
🔎 Types of Command Injection

Two main types exist.

1️⃣ Verbose Command Injection

The application directly shows command output.

Example payload:

whoami

Output example:

www-data

This makes exploitation easier.

2️⃣ Blind Command Injection

The application does not show command output.

Attackers must detect success indirectly.

Methods include:

Time delay

File creation

External requests

⏱ Detecting Blind Injection (Time Delay)

Example payload:

sleep 5

If the website pauses for 5 seconds, the command executed successfully.

Another method
ping -c 5 127.0.0.1

The server delays due to the ping command.

📡 Output Redirection

Attackers may redirect output to a file.

Example:

whoami > output.txt

Then read it:

cat output.txt
🧰 Useful Command Injection Payloads
Linux Payloads
Payload	Purpose
whoami	Show running user
ls	List files
ping	Time delay testing
sleep	Delay execution
nc	Reverse shell
Windows Payloads
Payload	Purpose
whoami	Show running user
dir	List directory
ping	Delay testing
timeout	Delay testing
🧪 Example Exploit

Payload:

; whoami

Application executes:

command ; whoami

Result:

www-data

Meaning the web server runs under the www-data user.

🧰 Using cURL for Testing

Example request:

curl http://target.app/search.php?search=Beatles;whoami

The injected command executes on the server.

🛡 Preventing Command Injection

Several methods exist.

1️⃣ Avoid Dangerous Functions

Avoid functions such as:

exec()
system()
passthru()

Use safer alternatives when possible.

2️⃣ Input Sanitization

Filter user input to allow only safe characters.

Example:

Only numbers allowed

Reject inputs containing:

; & | > < $
3️⃣ Input Validation

Allow only expected formats.

Example:

numbers only
4️⃣ Least Privilege

Run applications with minimal permissions.

Example:

www-data

Not root.

🧪 Practical Lab

Goal:

Read flag from /home/tryhackme/flag.txt

Example payload:

; cat /home/tryhackme/flag.txt
Application User
www-data
Flag
THM{COMMAND_INJECTION_COMPLETE}
⚠ Real-World Impact

Command injection can allow attackers to:

Read system files

Access databases

Modify server files

Install malware

Gain reverse shells

Take full control of the server

Because of this, command injection often leads to full system compromise.

🎯 Key Takeaways

Command Injection allows execution of OS commands.

Occurs when user input is not sanitized.

Can lead to Remote Code Execution (RCE).

Two main types: Blind and Verbose.

Prevention requires strict input validation and avoiding unsafe functions.

